1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,628
Welcome to the build!

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:05,255
First, the adapter case.

3
00:00:05,500 --> 00:00:09,903
The workspace fills with parts: the
circuit board and the housing.

4
00:00:13,500 --> 00:00:18,193
The board slots into place, with vents and
connectors lining up.

5
00:00:23,500 --> 00:00:27,090
And the finished case comes together, lid
and all.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:32,975
Every board and standoff has its place.

7
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:42,123
Next, the firmware that turns RC signals
into a joystick.

8
00:00:42,323 --> 00:00:47,283
We include the Joystick library and define
the throttle and steering pins.

9
00:00:47,483 --> 00:00:52,676
In setup, we enable the pins, then set the
X and Y axis ranges.

10
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:59,390
And in the loop, pulseIn reads each
channel and feeds it to the joystick axes.

11
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:03,988
Now, let's test it.

12
00:01:04,188 --> 00:01:08,660
Out on track, our 3D-printed wheel rim
does the steering.

13
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:19,241
And it works! RACELOGIC timing keeps score
as the laps come in.
